A||N||2||d825077||Break Question||||1||
Z||000000||d825077||11-25-2000||10:38 PM||||When I step on the breaks of my 1984 fiero, the peddle goes down farther than it used to.  If I step on the break a second time it will not go down as far as the first and stop.  The fluid is full, and all of the pads have less than 10,000 miles on them.  Any suggestions on where to start?  Should I bleed the breaks?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||11-25-2000||11:08 PM||||bleeding may help but the master cylinder is likely on it's way out.<P>when you replace it purge all the old fluid. bleed the wheels untill new fluid flows. If you don't you'll leave junk in the system that can trash the new master.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bcibob670||11-25-2000||11:09 PM||||yes bleed the brakes .   the need to press the brakes a second time is due to the air in the line which compresses at a difierent level compared to the brake fluid , creating a swuishy feeling or lack of initial pressure.  the density of one element is lighter than the other <P>BOB<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
